00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.860
Let's look at one simple grammar idea.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:24.520
Russian nouns have gender that affect endings.

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:26.360
Кошка ends in a.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:27.400
Они ошла вюд.

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:28.620
So it is feminine.

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:30.320
Саблака is also feminine.

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:32.540
Медведь ends in a consonant.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:34.900
So it is masculine compared to English.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:37.420
Where the cat stays the same no matter what.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:42.200
In Russian the word itself changes slightly with articles we don't even have.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:44.180
In Russian fairy tales.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.380
Animals often talk and help children.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:50.080
Like the clever fox or the vice bear in stories by pushkin.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:56.160
Russian love keeping cats as pets because they are seen as symbols of comfort and good luck in the home.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:58.260
Now it is practice time.

00:02:58.700 --> 00:03:02.720
For kids draw your favorite animal and write its Russian name underneath.

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:05.580
Then show it to a grown-up and say the word out loud.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.960
For adults look at a pet photo or zoo picture and describe it using three new words.

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:11.820
For example,

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:14.240
моя кошка or рыба в воде.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.580
Now hear something really cool about one of today's words.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:22.360
Did you know the Russian word кошка and the English word cat are actually cousins

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.020
that both trace back to the same ancient route.

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:30.520
This word started in North Africa or the Middle East with the sound keep or keep.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.900
Travel through Latin cactus, then into many European languages and finally reach the Russian as кошка.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:42.040
But be careful, the Russian word code is the masculine form for a male cat while кошка

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:44.840
is usually for a female cat or cat in general.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.260
Next time you see a cat, remember its Russian cousin.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.500
кошка comes from the same ancient meow sound route.

00:03:52.040 --> 00:03:55.120
That connection is your secret shortcut to remember it forever.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.500
That means well done.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:02.520
Remember every expert started as a beginner practicing today's words out loud,

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.600
even just once and you will be amazed how fast you learn.

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:06.860
See you next time.

